"11_worker.mst — the Smalltalk face of the worker doctrine (DolphinDart DD10).

 `docs/WORKERS.md` settles the doctrine; this is its image side.

     Worker do: #slowSum with: 1000000 then: [ :r | model value: r ]

 answers immediately. The work runs in a Dart ISOLATE — no shared state, no
 UI, data in and data out — and the continuation runs LATER, on the UI thread,
 out of `UiSession`'s posted-action queue.

 WHY THE BLOCK NEVER LEAVES THE IMAGE. Isolates copy; a Smalltalk block cannot
 cross and should not try. So the block stays here under an id, and only the
 id, the task name and the argument travel. The reply carries the id back and
 this class finds the block again.

 WHY THE RUN LOOP IS DART'S. `UiSession pump` drains Win32 messages and
 returns; it does not run Dart's event loop, so an isolate reply can only be
 delivered when control is back in Dart. The outer loop therefore belongs to
 Dart — pump a slice, yield, repeat — and `takePending` is how it collects the
 submissions this class has queued. See `test/worker_host.dart`.

 WHY `complete:with:` POSTS RATHER THAN CALLS. Calling the continuation
 directly would work, look correct, and violate the whole point: a
 continuation must not run inside a door entry (DD7 — a handler must not
 rebuild the window it is being called from, and a raise inside an entry
 cannot be caught outside it). Posting defers it to the next pump, where
 `UiSession`'s own containment covers it."
Object subclass: Worker [
    <classVars: Blocks Pending NextId Completed Failed>

    Worker class >> reset [
        Blocks := Dictionary new. Pending := OrderedCollection new.
        NextId := 0. Completed := 0. Failed := 0
    ]
    Worker class >> blocks [ Blocks isNil ifTrue: [ self reset ]. ^Blocks ]
    Worker class >> pending [ Pending isNil ifTrue: [ self reset ]. ^Pending ]

    "Submit work. Answers the id immediately — nothing here blocks."
    Worker class >> do: aTaskName with: anArgument then: aBlock [
        | id |
        self blocks.
        NextId := NextId + 1.
        id := NextId.
        Blocks at: id put: aBlock.
        Pending add: (Array with: id with: aTaskName displayString with: anArgument).
        ^id
    ]

    "How many submissions are waiting to be picked up, and how many are out
     with an isolate and not yet answered. The gate asserts on both: a task
     that was never collected and a task that never came back look identical
     from the outside otherwise."
    Worker class >> pendingCount [ ^self pending size ]
    Worker class >> inFlight [ ^self blocks size ]
    Worker class >> completed [ ^Completed ifNil: [ 0 ] ]
    Worker class >> failed [ ^Failed ifNil: [ 0 ] ]

    "The Dart host drains this. Answers an Array of {id. taskName. arg} and
     empties the queue — a submission is handed out exactly once."
    Worker class >> takePending [
        | out |
        out := self pending asArray.
        Pending := OrderedCollection new.
        ^out
    ]

    "A reply. POSTS the continuation; see the class comment."
    Worker class >> complete: anId with: aResult [
        | block |
        block := self blocks at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        block isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        Blocks removeKey: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        Completed := self completed + 1.
        ^UiSession postAction: [ block value: aResult ]
    ]

    "A task that raised inside the isolate. The failure is data by the time it
     reaches here — an exception cannot cross an isolate boundary either — so
     it arrives as a message and is posted the same way, letting the
     continuation's own handler deal with it."
    Worker class >> fail: anId with: aMessage [
        | block |
        block := self blocks at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        block isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        Blocks removeKey: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        Failed := self failed + 1.
        ^UiSession postAction: [
            block value: (Error new messageText: aMessage) ]
    ]
]
